WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.870
Renting a GPU provides temporary access to accelerator hardware without purchasing,

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:06.689
installing, powering, and maintaining it.

00:00:06.689 --> 00:00:10.865
A user can provision a remote machine, run a model or data job, collect the results,

00:00:10.865 --> 00:00:12.140
and release the resource.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:15.075
This is valuable when the requirement is short-lived, uncertain,

00:00:15.075 --> 00:00:18.120
or beyond the memory and performance of available local systems.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.465
Common reasons include evaluating a larger model before buying hardware,

00:00:21.465 --> 00:00:25.860
completing a temporary fine-tuning or batch-processing job, absorbing a demand spike,

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.245
and giving a distributed team access to a consistent environment.

00:00:29.245 --> 00:00:32.760
Rental also converts a large capital purchase into operational spending.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:37.420
That flexibility is useful when utilization would be too low to justify ownership or when

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.970
the required GPU type may change between projects.

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:41.595
The trade is not free convenience.

00:00:41.595 --> 00:00:45.360
The renter gives up some physical control and accepts provider capacity limits,

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.590
network dependence, ongoing metered charges, and responsibility for cloud identity,

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.140
access, and data handling.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.440
Setup time is billable.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.080
An idle instance can cost as much as a productive one.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.250
Uploading sensitive prompts or documents changes the data boundary,

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.590
and persistent disks or snapshots can outlive the compute session.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:05.640
Begin with a defined job: model, runtime, memory need, expected duration,

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.220
data classification, and acceptable interruption.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.120
Compare the complete rental cost with local capacity and managed alternatives.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.600
Renting succeeds when it delivers the needed compute for a controlled period and the

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.520
environment is shut down or deleted deliberately afterward.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.610
It fails when provisioning is easy enough that ownership, cost,

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:23.360
and residual data become invisible.
